+The independent observational assessment is limited to one airport station and remains exploratory. Point rainfall and regional-mean reanalysis have different spatial supports, and spatial averaging cannot be assumed to explain all distributional differences or model errors. No multi-station validation was implemented, and no independent station-based or radar-derived precipitation target was constructed at a spatial support matched to the 25-grid-point ERA5 regional mean. The station rainfall accumulation window remains uncertain under the adopted timestamp alignment. All 26,300 matched station observations carried rainfall indicator 111 (``investigation required''), with no indicator-0 (``satisfactory'') subset, so an indicator-0 sensitivity analysis could not be undertaken. This does not establish why code 111 occurs or imply that every amount is erroneous, but it limits the strength of observational validation. The XGBoost station-label experiment used regional ERA5 inputs and unchanged hyperparameters, without station-specific predictors or tuning, multi-station training, or deep-learning retraining on observations. Neither the exploratory observational comparison nor the non-significant label-training difference establishes transferability to other stations, regions or temporal scales. Our conclusions remain restricted to ERA5-derived regional-mean precipitation over Dublin. \revminor{Independent evaluations have documented differences between ERA5 precipitation and rain-gauge measurements, including biases in daily precipitation and underrepresentation of the largest observed daily totals \citep{lavers2022evaluation}. Although these findings concern daily precipitation across a much broader geographical domain, they reinforce the need to distinguish ERA5-referenced forecast evaluation from validation against local observations.}
